[candidate · 0:09] Okay, so the headline is that revenue is actually growing 15% but margin collapsed from 24% to 6% — that's an 18 point drop. So this is almost certainly a cost story rather than a demand story, though I want to check whether revenue growth came from new stores versus same-store sales, because opening locations can drag margin temporarily.

I'd structure it as profit equals revenue minus costs. On revenue, I'd look at price per transaction and transaction volume, and mix across drinks, food, retail beans. On costs, I'd split fixed versus variable — rent, labor, equipment, corporate overhead on the fixed side, and coffee beans, dairy, cups, and store-level labor on the variable side.

Given revenue is up but margin is down sharply, I'd prioritize the cost side first, and specifically look for one or two line items that ballooned.

