[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:05] Understood. Beyond pricing, what else could Brew & Bean do to rebuild its margin? Give me as many distinct ideas as you can.

[candidate · 1:13] A few buckets.

Procurement: consolidate volume across all 200 stores into fewer suppliers for better terms; forward-contract or hedge green coffee rather than buying spot; dual-source dairy to create competitive tension; negotiate packaging down, since cups and lids are commoditized and switchable.

Product and mix: push customers toward higher-margin items — brewed coffee and espresso carry far less dairy than a large latte. Promote food attach, and steer toward non-dairy or house-brand alternatives if the cost differential favors it. Reformulate portion sizes slightly rather than raising sticker price.

Waste: tighten par levels and forecasting, since dairy is perishable. Even a few points of spoilage recovery is real money at a 43-point input base.

Revenue-side, non-price: loyalty programs to lift visit frequency, and pulling delivery orders onto owned channels to avoid platform commissions.

Overhead: it drifted 12 to 14 — worth reviewing corporate headcount and whether expansion costs are being expensed.

Footprint: close or renegotiate leases on the bottom decile of stores.
